Mini-Project / Assignment
Building a LAN in Cisco Packet Tracer

Student Name	Kartik Nitin Pagar
PRN	2125UCSM1068
University	Sanjivani University
Subject / Unit	Unit 1: Introduction to Networking and Data Communication
 
1. Aim
To design and configure a Local Area Network (LAN) using Cisco Packet Tracer with 1 router, 2 switches, and 4 PCs, assign static IP addresses, and verify communication between devices using the ping command.
2. Objectives
•	Create a LAN topology using one router, two switches, and four PCs.
•	Connect the devices using appropriate Ethernet connections.
•	Configure static IPv4 addresses on all PCs.
•	Configure the router interfaces for communication between the two networks.
•	Test connectivity by sending ping packets between PCs connected through different switches.
3. Devices / Components Used
•	Cisco 2911 Router – 1
•	Cisco 2960-24TT Switch – 2
•	PC-PT – 4
•	Copper Ethernet cables
•	Cisco Packet Tracer
4. Network Topology
The following topology was created in Cisco Packet Tracer. The router connects the two switches, and each switch connects two PCs.
 

5. IP Addressing
The addressing used for the two LAN segments is shown below. The gateway addresses represent the router interfaces for the corresponding networks.
Device	IP Address	Subnet Mask	Default Gateway
PC0	192.168.1.2	255.255.255.0	192.168.1.1
PC1	192.168.1.3	255.255.255.0	192.168.1.1
PC2	192.168.2.2	255.255.255.0	192.168.2.1
PC3	192.168.2.3	255.255.255.0	192.168.2.1
6. Configuration Procedure
Step 1 – Place the devices
Open Cisco Packet Tracer and place one 2911 router, two 2960 switches, and four PCs in the workspace.
Step 2 – Connect the devices
Connect Router1 to both switches. Connect PC0 and PC1 to Switch0, and PC2 and PC3 to Switch1 using Ethernet cables.
Step 3 – Configure the PCs
For each PC, open Desktop → IP Configuration and select Static. Enter the IP address, subnet mask, and default gateway according to the addressing table above.
Step 4 – Configure the router
Configure the two router interfaces with 192.168.1.1/24 and 192.168.2.1/24 respectively, then enable the interfaces using the no shutdown command.
Step 5 – Verify connectivity
Open a PC command prompt and use the ping command to test communication. A successful reply confirms that packets are reaching the destination.
7. Connectivity Test
A ping test was performed to verify communication with the PC having IP address 192.168.2.2. The result shows four replies received, zero packets lost, and an average response time of approximately 1 ms.


8. Result
The LAN topology was successfully created in Cisco Packet Tracer. Static IP addresses were assigned to the PCs, and connectivity was verified using the ping command. The successful ping with 0% packet loss demonstrates that the configured devices can communicate across the network.
9. Conclusion
This mini-project demonstrates the basic operation of a routed LAN using Cisco Packet Tracer. It provides practical understanding of IP addressing, switching, routing, default gateways, and network connectivity testing.

